Hypervisor:
    Software managing virtual machines
    Handles communication between guest and host / hardware

    Type 1 bare-metal
        Hypervisor is the "root" sitting on the hardware.
        Calls the hardware directly with no host OS.

        ex. Virtual private servers

            Guest <-> Hypervisor <-> Hardware

        Bare metal as in the hypervisor is bare metal
        NOT the same as bare metal servers i.e. Vultr Bare metal / Hetzner dedicated (No hypervisor)

    Type 2 hosted
        Hypervisor sits between the gues and host OS'

        ex. Virtual machines

            Guest <-> Hypervisor <-> Host OS <-> Hardware

Virtualization:
    Full virtualization:
        Hypervisor "pretends" to be hardware
        Guest system doesn't know it's virtualized.

    Hardware-assisted virtualization:
         Hardware accelerated virtualization using instructions of modern CPU architectures.

    Paravirtualization:
        Guest knows it is virtualized and communicates directly with hypervisor 
        Typically faster than full virtualization. 
        Uses specialized drivers
        partially obsolete in favor of hardware assisted virtualization

    OS-Level virtualization (containerization)
        Don't require hypervisors (unless host kernel is incompatible with guest)
            i.e. linux containers on windows
        Shared kernel space with host, Isolated user space
        docker, podman

    Emulation
        Entire hardware simulated in host OS user space software.
        Slow, mainly used to simulated old or weaker architectures
        Dosbox, console emulators



Side channel attacks:
    Attacker can inferr information from "metadata"
    Leaked shared caches
    timings of shared resources

